From: postmarketOS rhodep port
Subject: [PATCH] rpmsg: glink: give the rcid entry and the endpoint a
 reference each

qcom_glink_alloc_channel() takes one reference, and which object owns it
depends on how the channel came into being. There are three cases, and the
driver treats the third one as if it were the second.

  channel opened by the remote (qcom_glink_rx_open with create_device)
      the allocation reference belongs to the rcids entry, as that function's
      own error path states: "Release the reference, iff we took it". The
      lcids entry gets its own from the kref_get in send_open_req. Two
      references, two idr entries, and rx_close and rx_close_ack put one each.
      Balanced.

  the rpmsg_chrdev channel (qcom_glink_create_chrdev)
      the allocation reference belongs to the rpmsg_device, and
      qcom_glink_device_release puts it. The channel is in neither idr.
      Balanced.

  channel opened from this side (qcom_glink_create_local, which is what
  rpmsg_create_ept and therefore rpmsg_char's RPMSG_CREATE_EPT_IOCTL reach)
      the allocation reference belongs to the endpoint. There is no
      rpmsg_device, so nothing releases it. The lcids entry gets the
      send_open_req reference, and when the remote answers, rx_open finds the
      channel already in lcids and adds it to rcids without taking anything.
      Two references and two idr entries, but one of those references is the
      endpoint's.

That last case only reaches zero by having rx_close() put back a reference the
rcids entry never took, spending the endpoint's. It survives the ordinary
close, where the endpoint is being destroyed anyway, and comes apart whenever
the endpoint outlives the channel.

qcom_glink_native_remove() puts one reference per lcids entry and one per
rcids entry, which for such a channel is both of them, so the channel is freed
while rpmsg_char still holds a pointer to its endpoint:

	refcount_t: underflow; use-after-free.
	WARNING: lib/refcount.c:28 at refcount_warn_saturate+0xd8/0x13c
	Workqueue: rproc_recovery_wq rproc_crash_handler_work
	Call trace:
	 refcount_warn_saturate+0xd8/0x13c (P)
	 qcom_glink_native_remove+0x160/0x268
	 qcom_glink_smem_unregister+0x28/0x934 [qcom_glink_smem]
	 glink_subdev_stop+0x1c/0x30 [qcom_common]
	 rproc_stop+0xb4/0x144
	 rproc_boot_recovery / rproc_trigger_recovery / rproc_crash_handler_work

and the ordinary close path reaches the same end without any of remoteproc
being involved, which is easier to see. Freeing the channel leaves the pointer
in the rcids idr, and the next qcom_glink_create_ept() walks that idr
comparing names:

	[  191.909585] close ack on unknown channel
	[  885.474811] rpmsg rpmsg0: failed to open DS
	[  925.532523] Unable to handle kernel paging request at
	                        virtual address 0000000000010000
	[  925.534463] Internal error: Oops: 0000000096000006 [#1] SMP
	               pc : __pi_strcmp+0x24/0x140
	               lr : qcom_glink_create_ept+0x74/0x2dc
	               x0 : 0000000000010000   x3 : 0000000000005344
	Call trace:
	 __pi_strcmp+0x24/0x140 (P)
	 rpmsg_create_ept+0x48/0x78
	 rpmsg_eptdev_open+0x9c/0x124 [rpmsg_char]
	 chrdev_open+0x16c/0x200
	 do_dentry_open / vfs_open / path_openat / do_sys_openat2
	 __arm64_sys_openat+0x70/0x9c

x3 holds 0x5344, the "DS" being looked for; x0 is what is left of the name it
was compared against. Root is needed to open the character device, but nothing
beyond that: two open() calls on /dev/rpmsgN are enough.

Give the rcids entry its own reference, and release the endpoint's when the
endpoint is destroyed, so that every reference has exactly one owner:

  local channel   alloc 1 endpoint, send_open_req 1 lcids, rx_open 1 rcids
                  destroy_ept -1, rx_close_ack -1, rx_close -1 -> 0

  remote channel  unchanged at 2, and destroy_ept must not put, because there
                  the allocation reference belongs to the rcids entry rather
                  than to the endpoint. channel->rpdev cannot be used to tell
                  them apart, since qcom_glink_remove_rpmsg_device() has
                  already cleared it by that point and does so for both, hence
                  the explicit flag.

Teardown works out because qcom_glink_native_remove() destroys the child
rpmsg devices, and with them the endpoints, before it walks the idrs: the
endpoint's reference goes first, then one per idr entry, reaching zero with
nothing left pointing at the channel.

Tested on a Motorola Moto G82 5G (SM6375) running v7.2-rc5. Before, opening an
endpoint on the modem's DS channel and reopening it froze the machine within
three attempts. After, 200 open/close cycles complete in 1.3 s with no warning
and no Oops.

--- a/drivers/rpmsg/qcom_glink_native.c
+++ b/drivers/rpmsg/qcom_glink_native.c
@@ -168,6 +168,17 @@
 
 	struct kref refcount;
 
+	/*
+	 * Set for channels created by qcom_glink_create_local(), i.e. those
+	 * opened from this side through rpmsg_create_ept(). Such a channel has
+	 * no rpmsg_device backing it, so the reference taken by
+	 * qcom_glink_alloc_channel() belongs to the endpoint and has to be
+	 * released when the endpoint is destroyed. For a channel opened by the
+	 * remote that same reference belongs to the rcids entry, and for the
+	 * rpmsg_chrdev channel it belongs to the rpmsg_device.
+	 */
+	bool local_ept;
+
 	spinlock_t recv_lock;
 
 	char *name;
@@ -1267,6 +1278,8 @@
 
 	qcom_glink_send_open_ack(glink, channel);
 
+	channel->local_ept = true;
+
 	return channel;
 
 err_timeout:
@@ -1422,6 +1435,12 @@
 	qcom_glink_remove_rpmsg_device(glink, channel);
 
 	qcom_glink_send_close_req(glink, channel);
+
+	if (channel->local_ept) {
+		channel->local_ept = false;
+		/* Release qcom_glink_create_local()'s reference */
+		kref_put(&channel->refcount, qcom_glink_channel_release);
+	}
 }
 
 static int qcom_glink_request_intent(struct qcom_glink *glink,
@@ -1659,6 +1678,15 @@
 	trace_qcom_glink_cmd_open_rx(glink->label, name, channel->lcid, rcid);
 
 	spin_lock_irqsave(&glink->idr_lock, flags);
+	if (!create_device) {
+		/*
+		 * The channel was already in lcids, so the reference from
+		 * qcom_glink_alloc_channel() is spoken for. Take one for the
+		 * rcids entry, which qcom_glink_rx_close() and
+		 * qcom_glink_native_remove() will both put back.
+		 */
+		kref_get(&channel->refcount);
+	}
 	ret = idr_alloc(&glink->rcids, channel, rcid, rcid + 1, GFP_ATOMIC);
 	if (ret < 0) {
 		dev_err(glink->dev, "Unable to insert channel into rcid list\n");
@@ -1703,9 +1731,8 @@
 	channel->rcid = 0;
 	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&glink->idr_lock, flags);
 free_channel:
-	/* Release the reference, iff we took it */
-	if (create_device)
-		kref_put(&channel->refcount, qcom_glink_channel_release);
+	/* Either the allocation reference or the one taken for rcids above */
+	kref_put(&channel->refcount, qcom_glink_channel_release);
 
 	return ret;
 }
